<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 2: Multilevel Data                                       -->
<!--                                                                    -->
<!--  Conceptual chapter, no computation. The Berkeley admissions case  -->
<!--  is real and properly citable (Bickel, Hammel & O'Connell 1975),   -->
<!--  as is the kidney stone case (Bonovas & Piovani 2023). The Kenya   -->
<!--  tie-in (ss-kenya-relevance) is also a real, sourced fact - KUCCPS' -->
<!--  own affirmative action criteria - not an invented case study.    -->
<!--  Deliberately not tied to MICS or the flamingo dataset as the     -->
<!--  running example.                                                 -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-multilevel-data" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Multilevel Data</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-multidimensional-data">
    <p>
      Most datasets contain more than one kind of thing.
    </p>

    <p>
      A hospital records patients — but also the wards those patients are
      admitted to, and the hospitals those wards belong to.
      A school survey collects responses from students — but those students sit
      in classrooms, and those classrooms are in schools, and those schools are
      in districts.
      A flamingo study measures individual birds — but those birds belong to
      flocks, and those flocks live in different lakes.
    </p>

    <p>
      When data has this kind of layered structure, the structure is not just
      background detail.
      It changes what questions you can ask, what comparisons are valid, and
      what conclusions you are entitled to draw.
      Ignore it, and you can produce results that are arithmetically correct but
      analytically wrong — as one of this chapter's examples will show rather
      dramatically.
    </p>

    <p>
      This chapter is about learning to see that structure before you start
      analysing.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 1: Berkeley                                               -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-berkeley-admissions">
    <title>A Question About Fairness</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In the early 1970s, concern was growing about gender equality in higher
        education in the United States.
        Against this backdrop, an analysis of admissions data from the
        University of California, Berkeley caught public attention.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-berkeley-the-numbers">
      <title>The numbers seemed clear</title>

      <p>
        In 1973, UC Berkeley faced a lawsuit over sex bias when overall graduate
        admissions showed 44.3% of men but only 34.6% of women were accepted
        across the whole university, This was in the same institution, and in
        the same year.
        Newspapers reported the figures, and public concern followed.
        It appeared that women were being treated unfairly.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-berkeley-overall-admissions">
        <caption>
          University-wide graduate admission rates at UC Berkeley in 1973, by
          sex: 44.3% of male applicants were admitted, compared with 34.6% of
          female applicants.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/berkeley_img_1.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A bar chart with two bars comparing overall graduate admission
              rates at UC Berkeley in 1973.
              The bar for male applicants reaches 44.3%, noticeably taller than
              the bar for female applicants, which reaches 34.6%.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Before any action was taken, the researchers dug deeper into the
        numbers.
        Rather than asking simply <em>what</em> the numbers showed, they asked
        <em>how</em> those decisions were actually made.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-berkeley-how-decisions-are-made">
      <title>One university, many decisions</title>

      <p>
        Admissions at Berkeley were not decided by a single central committee.
        Instead, each academic department made their own decisions, with their
        own standards, number of places, and level of competition.
      </p>

      <p>
        While correct, the university-wide figures only told part of the story.
        Instead, we can look at the exact same data and information, but just
        look at it by department, instead of at the whole university level.
        Now were women less likely to be admitted than men?
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-berkeley-admissions-by-dept">
        <caption>
          Graduate admission rates at UC Berkeley in 1973, by department and
          sex.
          The rates for male and female applicants vary by department.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/berkeley_img_by_dept.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A bar chart with two bars for each department comparing graduate
              admission rates at UC Berkeley in 1973.
              The bar for male applicants reaches a certain percentage,
              noticeably taller or shorter than the bar for female applicants,
              depending on the department.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-berkeley-what-they-found">
      <title>What they found</title>

      <p>
        In most departments, women were admitted around the same rate as men.
        In fact, in several departments women were admitted at a <em>higher</em>
        rate!
        The apparent discrimination had all but vanished.
      </p>

      <p>
        So how could women have a lower acceptance rate overall, if they were
        not being disadvantaged within any individual department?
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-berkeley-admissions-total-applicants-by-dept">
        <caption>
          Number of graduate applicants at UC Berkeley in 1973, by department
          and sex.
          The numbers for male and female applicants vary by department.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/berkeley_img_total_applicants.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A bar chart with two bars for each department comparing the number
              of graduate applicants at UC Berkeley in 1973.
              The bar for male applicants reaches a certain number, noticeably
              taller or shorter than the bar for female applicants, depending on
              the department.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The answer lay in where people applied.
        On average, women tended to apply to departments that were more
        competitive <mdash/> departments with fewer places and lower acceptance
        rates for everyone, regardless of sex.
        Men, on average, applied to departments with higher acceptance rates.
        Once every department was blended into one set of university- wide
        numbers, that difference in <em>where people applied</em> disappeared
        from view, and only a difference in <em>who got in</em> was left
        visible.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-berkeley-lesson">
        <title>
          The university was not discriminating.
          The way the numbers were combined was.
        </title>

        <p>
          By looking at applications across the entire university, unlike
          situations were treated as if they were the same.
          It blurred two distinct questions: how applicants were treated
          <em>within</em> a department, and how departments differed
          <em>from one another</em>.
        </p>

        <p>
          What is important is that this was not a mistake in arithmetic.
          But, instead, the mistake was in how the situation was understood.
          The data reflected a layered reality <mdash/> applicants belonged to
          departments, and departments belonged to a university <mdash/> and
          flattening that layered structure into one set of averages erased
          exactly the context needed to read it correctly.
        </p>
      </insight>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-berkeley-name-and-stakes">
      <title>Simpson's paradox</title>

      <p>
        This pattern <mdash/> a trend that appears in combined data but reverses
        or vanishes once the data is broken into its natural groups <mdash/> is
        now known as <term>Simpson's paradox</term>
        <idx><h>Simpson's paradox</h></idx>.
        It is one of the most famous cautionary tales in applied statistics, and
        for good reason: the consequences at Berkeley were not trivial.
        A university's reputation was at stake, a serious accusation had been
        made in public, and it took careful, patient re-analysis to show that
        the accusation, though built from real numbers, did not hold up.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-simpsons-paradox">
        <caption>A GIF Illustrating Simpson's Paradox</caption>
        <image source="images/simpsons_paradox.gif" width="100%"/>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The deeper lesson is simpler than the paradox's reputation suggests.
        Multilevel data is not about advanced models or complicated equations.
        It is about recognising how the world is actually organised, and
        refusing to erase that organisation just because a single table of
        averages is easier to report.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Bickel, P.J., Hammel, E.A.
        and O'Connell, J.W.
        (1975).
        <q>Sex Bias in Graduate Admissions: Data from Berkeley.</q>
        <em>Science</em>, 187(4175), pp.398 <ndash/> 404.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-multilevel-structures">
      <title>Multilevel Structures</title>

      <p>
        There are many examples of multilevel data structures:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Pupils (Level 1) within classrooms (Level 2) within schools (Level
              3).
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Repeated measurements (Level 1) within individuals (Level 2).
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Households (Level 1) within villages (Level 2) within districts
              (Level 3).
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Employees within teams within organisations.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Patients within wards within hospitals.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        Then more here.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-kenya-relevance">
      <title>Why this matters here</title>

      <p>
        This is a story from 1970s America.
        Why is this important here?
        Kenya's own university placement body, the Kenya Universities and
        Colleges Central Placement Service (KUCCPS), applies an approved
        <term>Affirmative Action Criteria</term> covering gender, persons with
        disabilities and marginalised regions when placing students into higher
        education programmes.
        The aim is to enhance access to higher education for groups that may
        otherwise be disadvantaged.
        (KUCCPS, n.d.)
      </p>

      <p>
        The principle is familiar: a single overall number can hide important
        differences between groups.
        A national placement rate, or a single measure of performance, may tell
        you something useful about the system as a whole while telling you very
        little about how that system is working for particular groups.
        The Berkeley example shows why this matters.
        The overall numbers looked very different from the numbers within
        individual departments.
        The lesson is not that every difference between groups is evidence of
        unfairness, nor that every difference is an aggregation effect.
        It is that a pooled statistic is a starting point, not necessarily the
        end of the analysis.
      </p>

      <p>
        So, when you encounter a national placement rate, an examination result,
        or any other pooled statistic, ask yourself:
        <em>what groups have been
  combined to produce this number?</em> Break
        the data down, look again, and only then start drawing conclusions.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Kenya Universities and Colleges Central Placement
        Service (KUCCPS).
        <q>Frequently Asked Questions.</q>
        <url href="https://kuccps.net/frequently-asked-questions" visual="kuccps.net/frequently-asked-questions"/>.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 2: it happened again, elsewhere                          -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-kidney-stones">
    <title>Aside: This Wasn't a One-Off</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        TODO: Does this example link to Multilevel data?
        Or instead, is this more like factors/grouping.
      </p>

      <p>
        If Berkeley sounds like a coincidence of the numbers, here is a second,
        unrelated case where exactly the same thing happened, a decade later, in
        a completely different field.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-kidney-two-treatments">
      <title>Two treatments for kidney stones</title>

      <p>
        In the early 1980s, surgeons were deciding how best to treat kidney
        stones.
        There were two options.
        The first was open surgery: invasive, difficult, but familiar.
        The other was newer and far less invasive, a procedure called
        percutaneous nephrolithotomy, promising quicker recovery and, many
        hoped, better results.
      </p>

      <p>
        Looking at outcomes across all patients, the answer seemed clear: the
        newer procedure appeared to work better overall.
        More patients were successfully treated.
        On the surface, it looked like the obvious choice.
      </p>

      <p>
        TODO: Graph or numbers of the overall numbers
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-kidney-stone-size">
      <title>Not all stones are equal</title>

      <p>
        But kidney stones are not all the same.
        Some are small and relatively easy to remove; others are large,
        multiple, or deeply embedded, and far harder to treat.
        When researchers separated patients into two groups <mdash/> small
        stones and large stones <mdash/> the story changed completely.
      </p>

      <p>
        TODO: Graph or numbers of split by stone size
      </p>

      <p>
        Within each group, open surgery had the <em>higher</em> success rate.
        It worked better for patients with small stones, and it also worked
        better for patients with large stones.
        The treatment that looked worse overall was performing better in every
        comparable situation.
      </p>

      <p>
        The contradiction came from how the treatments were actually used in
        practice.
        Patients with small, easy stones were far more likely to receive the
        newer, less invasive procedure.
        Patients with large or complex stones were far more likely to be sent
        for open surgery.
        Lump all patients together, and the procedure that had been handed the
        hardest cases ends up looking like the weaker one, even though, case for
        comparable case, it was the more effective option.
      </p>

      <p>
        Patients are grouped by stone size, and the conclusion reverses
        depending on which level you look at: the whole patient group, or the
        two size-based groups within it.
        Ignoring that grouping doesn't simplify the analysis.
        It distorts it.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Bonovas, S.
        and Piovani, D.
        (2023).
        <q>Simpson's
        Paradox in Clinical Research: A Cautionary Tale.</q>
        <em>Journal of
        Clinical Medicine</em>, 12(4), 1633.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

