  <section xml:id="sec-mics-multilevel">
    <title>Multilevel Data in R: Kenya MICS</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The Kenya Multiple Indicator Cluster Survey (MICS) is a nationally
        representative household survey conducted by UNICEF and the Kenya
        National Bureau of Statistics.
        The survey, like most modern data, was designed around a multilevel
        structure: children are nested within women, women are nested within
        households, and households are nested within geographic clusters.
      </p>

      <p>
        The data is freely available but requires registration.
        To follow the R code in this section:
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Go to
              <url href="https://mics.unicef.org/surveys" visual="mics.unicef.org/surveys"/>
              and select Kenya.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Register for a free account and request access to the microdata.
              Access is typically granted within a few days.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Download the dataset and place the <c>.sav</c> files in your
              working directory.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        If you do not yet have access, read through this section for the
        concepts.
        The ideas apply to any multilevel dataset.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mics-files">
      <title>Four files, four levels</title>

      <p>
        The Kenya MICS comes as four separate data files, each corresponding to
        a different level of the hierarchy:
      </p>

      <tabular halign="left">
        <col halign="left" width="20%"/>
        <col halign="left" width="20%"/>
        <col halign="right" width="15%"/>
        <col halign="left" width="45%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>File</cell>
          <cell>Level</cell>
          <cell>Rows</cell>
          <cell>What each row represents</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>hhKE.sav</c></cell>
          <cell>Household</cell>
          <cell>8,993</cell>
          <cell>One household</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>hlKE.sav</c></cell>
          <cell>Household member</cell>
          <cell>46,038</cell>
          <cell>One person living in a surveyed household</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>wmKE.sav</c></cell>
          <cell>Woman</cell>
          <cell>10,525</cell>
          <cell>One woman aged 15 <ndash/> 49 in a surveyed household</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>chKE.sav</c></cell>
          <cell>Child</cell>
          <cell>7,266</cell>
          <cell>One child under 5 in a surveyed household</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <p>
        The row counts are not multiples of each other - not every household has
        a child under five; and not every woman has children; and not every
        household member is a woman aged 15 to 49.
        The files represent overlapping but distinct subsets of the people in
        the survey.
      </p>

      <p>
        Load all four files in R using the <c>haven</c> package, which reads
        SPSS <c>.sav</c> files:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        library(haven)
        library(dplyr)

        hh &lt;- read_sav("hhKE.sav")   # households
        hl &lt;- read_sav("hlKE.sav")   # household members
        wm &lt;- read_sav("wmKE.sav")   # women
        ch &lt;- read_sav("chKE.sav")   # children under 5

        # How many rows in each file?
        nrow(hh)
        #&gt; [1] 8993
        nrow(hl)
        #&gt; [1] 46038
        nrow(wm)
        #&gt; [1] 10525
        nrow(ch)
        #&gt; [1] 7266
        </code>
      </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mics-variables">
      <title>Understanding the variable names</title>

      <p>
        Opening any of the four files, the column names are not immediately
        informative.
        The household file contains variables called <c>HI1</c>, <c>HI2</c>,
        <c>HI3D</c>, <c>HI3M</c>, <c>HI3Y</c>, <c>HI10</c>, <c>HIMEM</c>, and so
        on.
        These names follow a coding scheme common in large household surveys:
        short alphanumeric codes that are unambiguous and consistent across
        countries and survey rounds, but which tell you nothing about what the
        variable contains without consulting the documentation.
      </p>

      <p>
        The reason for this convention is practical.
        MICS surveys are conducted in dozens of countries, often translated into
        multiple languages.
        Short codes like <c>HI1</c> are language-neutral and do not change
        between the French version and the English version of the same survey.
        Long descriptive names would vary by language and make cross-country
        comparisons harder to automate.
        The cost is that the names are opaque to anyone who does not have the
        documentation.
      </p>

      <p>
        The Word document distributed with the Kenya MICS download provides the
        translation.
        <c>HI1</c> is the cluster number.
        <c>HI2</c> is the household number within the cluster.
        <c>HI3D</c>, <c>HI3M</c>, and <c>HI3Y</c> are the day, month, and year
        of interview.
        <c>HI10</c> is the result of the household interview.
        <c>HIMEM</c> is the number of household members.
        Refer to that document whenever an unfamiliar variable name appears.
      </p>

      <p>
        In R, the <c>haven</c> package preserves the variable labels from the
        <c>.sav</c> file, so you do not always need to look them up manually.
        The labels are attached to each column and can be viewed directly:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      # See the label for one variable
      attr(hh$HI1, "label")
      #&gt; [1] "Cluster number"

      attr(hh$HIMEM, "label")
      #&gt; [1] "Number of household members"

      # See labels for all variables at once
      labelled::var_label(hh)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The screenshot below shows what this looks like when the data is opened
        in a spreadsheet: the short variable codes appear as column headers,
        with the descriptive labels visible in a second row beneath them.
        In R, those labels are stored as metadata attached to each column rather
        than as a visible row, but they are always accessible.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-mics-variable-labels">
        <caption>
          Variable names and labels from the Kenya MICS household file, as
          displayed in a spreadsheet.
          Short codes like <c>HI1</c> and <c>HIMEM</c> are the actual column
          names; the descriptive labels below them are stored as metadata and
          preserved by the <c>haven</c> package when the file is read into R.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch2_mics_variable_labels.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A spreadsheet showing the top two rows of the Kenya MICS household
              file.
              The first row contains short variable codes: HI1, HI2, HI3D, HI3M,
              HI3Y, HI10, HIMEM.
              The second row contains descriptive labels in orange text: Cluster
              number, Household number, Day of interview, Month of interview,
              Year of interview, Result of household interview, Number of
              household members.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mics-linking">
      <title>How the levels connect, and where they do not</title>

      <p>
        The files are linked by a shared household identifier, <c>HID</c>.
        Every record in every file should be traceable back to a household in
        <c>hhKE.sav</c>.
        Let us check whether that is actually true.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      # How many unique households appear in each file?
      n_distinct(hh$HID)
      #&gt; [1] 8993
      n_distinct(hl$HID)
      #&gt; [1] 8989
      n_distinct(wm$HID)
      #&gt; [1] 7787
      n_distinct(ch$HID)
      #&gt; [1] 4955
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The household file has 8,993 households.
        The household members file has members from 8,989 of them.
        Four households that exist in the household file have no corresponding
        members.
        Does this mean four households were empty?
        We can investigate.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      # Which households are in hh but not in hl?
      missing_hids &lt;- setdiff(hh$HID, hl$HID)
      missing_hids
      #&gt; [1]      18043      76075     314034    1240005

      hh %&gt;%
        filter(HID %in% missing_hids) %&gt;%
        select(HID, HI13, HIMEM)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Looking at these four households reveals something interesting.
        Two have a large number of <c>NA</c> values across their records,
        including in the number of household members variable ( <c>HIMEM</c> ) —
        consistent with households that were visited but where no interview was
        completed.
        The sex and age fields for the head of household are coded 7 and 97
        respectively, which the data dictionary confirms are codes for missing
        rather than real values.
        These households may genuinely have had no members present at the time
        of the survey.
      </p>

      <p>
        But one of the four — household 314034 — has a non-missing value for the
        variable indicating an eligible child under 5.
        If there was an eligible child, there should have been a household
        member record.
        Let us check whether that child appears in the children's file:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      ch %&gt;% filter(HID %in% missing_hids)
      #&gt; # A tibble: 0 x ...
      #&gt; # ... with ... variables
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The child does not appear in the children's file either.
        This is a genuine inconsistency in the data: the household record says
        there should be a child, but neither the household members file nor the
        children's file contains any record of them.
        Whether this reflects a recording error, a missed interview, or
        something else is impossible to determine from the data alone.
      </p>

      <p>
        The women's file raises a different puzzle.
        Household 76075 is among the four with no household member records, but
        it appears in the women's file — twice, as expected, since the household
        record indicates two women aged 15 to 49.
        And household 1240005, also missing from the household members file, has
        a woman's record too.
        A woman exists in the women's file whose household has no members:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      wm %&gt;% filter(HID %in% missing_hids)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        This is internally inconsistent.
        A woman must be a household member, so a woman's record without a
        corresponding household member record is a data problem, not a genuine
        feature of the survey.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now check the other direction: are there women or children in the data
        whose household ID does not appear in the household file at all?
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      # Women whose HID does not appear in the household file
      setdiff(wm$HID, hh$HID)
      #&gt; [1] ... 183 values ...

      # Children whose HID does not appear in the household file
      setdiff(ch$HID, hh$HID)
      #&gt; integer(0)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        There are 183 women whose household ID does not match any household in
        the household file.
        The children's file, by contrast, has no such orphans — every child's
        household ID appears in the household file.
        The women's file has a more serious linkage problem than the children's
        file.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-mics-orphans">
        <title>Real multilevel data is rarely perfectly linked</title>

        <p>
          In a perfectly collected multilevel dataset, every record at every
          level would link cleanly to exactly one record at the level above it.
          In practice, this is almost never true.
          Records go missing.
          Identifiers are recorded incorrectly.
          Interviews are partially completed.
          The four households with no members, the woman with no household
          record, and the 183 women with unmatched household IDs are all
          examples of what is called <term>orphan records</term> — records that
          cannot be linked to their parent level.
          Finding and understanding orphan records is a normal part of working
          with multilevel survey data, and investigating them — as we did above
          — is more informative than simply discarding them.
          Sometimes an orphan record reveals a coding error that can be fixed.
          Sometimes it reveals a genuine gap in data collection.
          Sometimes, as with household 314034, it reveals an inconsistency that
          cannot be resolved from the data alone.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        The data cleaning work this kind of investigation requires is covered in
        <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>.
        For now, the important point is that identifying these issues is the
        first step — and the step that is only possible once you understand the
        multilevel structure of the data well enough to know what
        <q>correctly linked</q> should look like.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mics-trace">
      <title>Tracing one household through all four files</title>

      <p>
        A useful exercise when first encountering multilevel data is to trace
        one unit through every level.
        Pick one household and find every record that relates to it across all
        four files.
        This makes the structure concrete.
      </p>

      <p>
        To see all four files in action, we need a household that has at least
        one woman aged 15 to 49 and at least one child under 5.
        The variables <c>HI12</c> and <c>HI13</c> in the household file record
        these counts.
        We take the first household that satisfies both conditions:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      example_hid &lt;- (hh %&gt;%
        filter(HI12 &gt; 0 &amp; HI13 &gt; 0) %&gt;%
        pull(HID))[1]

      example_hid
      #&gt; [1] 2078
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Now pull every record related to household 2078 from each file:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
      # Household-level record
      hh %&gt;% filter(HID == example_hid)

      # Everyone in the household
      hl %&gt;% filter(HID == example_hid)
      #&gt; # A tibble: 7 x ...
      # Seven people live in this household.

      # Women aged 15-49 in the household
      wm %&gt;% filter(HID == example_hid)
      #&gt; # A tibble: 2 x ...
      # Two of those seven are women aged 15-49.

      # Children under 5 in the household
      ch %&gt;% filter(HID == example_hid)
      #&gt; # A tibble: 1 x ...
      # One child under 5.
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Household 2078 has seven members in total.
        Two of those seven are women aged 15 to 49, so they each have a record
        in the women's file.
        One is a child under 5, with a record in the children's file.
        The remaining four household members (perhaps older children, men, or
        elderly relatives) appear only in the household members file, nowhere
        else.
        We can verify their ages and sex by looking at the household members
        file.
        We can see from the four data sets information on household 2078:
        <ul>
          <li>
            The household record: They are in Central Kenya, in the 4th wealth
            quintile (where 5 is richest quintile), They have water piped into
            their house, and a traditional pit latrine.
          </li>

          <li>
            The women's record: Two women aged 18 and 25 reside in this
            household.
            They both have primary education level, and the 25 year old has
            given birth, and the 18 year-old has not.
          </li>

          <li>
            The under-5 record: One child under 5 - an 8 month old girl with
            some vaccinations.
            They are 7.2kg and 66cm.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Variables measured at the household level, such as water source, wealth
        index, or sanitation, apply to all seven members of this household
        equally.
        Variables measured at the woman level, such as education, or
        reproductive history, apply only to the two women.
        And the variables measured at the child level, such as height, weight,
        or vaccination status, apply only to the one child.
        To ask whether this child's nutritional status is related to the
        mother's education and the household's wealth requires linking across
        all three of those levels.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mics-level-mapping">
      <title>Mapping the levels</title>

      <p>
        Before analysing any multilevel dataset, it is worth doing a brief
        level-mapping exercise: identifying what units exist at each level, how
        they connect, and what variables are measured at each level.
        For the Kenya MICS, the map looks like this:
      </p>

      <tabular halign="left">
        <col halign="left" width="20%"/>
        <col halign="left" width="25%"/>
        <col halign="left" width="55%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>Level</cell>
          <cell>Unit</cell>
          <cell>Example variables measured here</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>4 (highest)</cell>
          <cell>Household</cell>
          <cell>
            Water source, Floor materials, Province, Wealth index, ...
          </cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>3</cell>
          <cell>Household Members</cell>
          <cell>Sex, Age, Education level, ...</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>2</cell>
          <cell>Woman</cell>
          <cell>
            Age, Education, Number of children born, care during pregnancy, ...
          </cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell>1 (lowest)</cell>
          <cell>Child under 5</cell>
          <cell>Age in months, Health, Vaccination history, ...</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <p>
        This mapping immediately raises questions that guide the analysis.
        If the outcome of interest is a child's nutritional status, e.g.,
        whether their height is low for their age, then the outcome lives at
        Level 1.
        But the potential explanations live at multiple levels: the child's own
        age and sex (Level 1), the mother's education (Level 2), the household's
        wealth and water source (Level 3), and whether the household is in an
        urban or rural area (Level 4).
        None of these levels can be ignored without potentially missing
        something important.
      </p>

      <p>
        The key questions to ask when mapping any multilevel dataset:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              How many units are there at each level?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              What variable links each level to the one above it?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Are group sizes roughly equal, or do some groups have many more
              members than others?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              What variables are measured at each level?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              At which level does the outcome of interest live, and at which
              levels do the potential explanatory variables live?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        # How many household members per household?
        hl %&gt;%
          group_by(Hid) %&gt;%
          summarise(n_members = n()) %&gt;%
          summarise(
            mean_size = mean(n_members),
            min_size  = min(n_members),
            max_size  = max(n_members)
          )
        #&gt; # A tibble: 1 x 3
        #&gt;   mean_size min_size max_size
        #&gt;       &lt;dbl&gt;    &lt;int&gt;    &lt;int&gt;
        #&gt; 1      5.12        1       23

        # How many children per household, among households
        # with at least one child?
        ch %&gt;%
          group_by(Hid) %&gt;%
          summarise(n_children = n()) %&gt;%
          count(n_children)
        #&gt; # A tibble: 6 x 2
        #&gt; n_children     n
        #&gt;      &lt;int&gt; &lt;int&gt;
        #&gt; 1  3040
        #&gt; 2  1579
        #&gt; 3   286
        #&gt; 4    42
        #&gt; 5     6
        #&gt; 6     2
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Unsurprisingly, household sizes are unequal — some households have one
        member, others many.
        This is typical of multilevel data from household surveys, and it
        matters for analysis: a household with eight members contributes more
        rows to the dataset than a household with two, which can distort simple
        summaries if the nesting is ignored.
        Looking at the mean number of rooms within a household using the `hh`
        data and comparing it to the `hl` data shows a slight difference:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        mean(hh$SE4, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 2.392829
        mean(hl$SE4, na.rm = TRUE)
        #&gt; [1] 2.507339
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The mean number of rooms per household is slightly higher when
        calculated from the household-level data (`hl`) compared to the
        individual-level data (`hh`).
        This difference arises because larger households are represented
        multiple times in the `hl` dataset (there is one row per household
        member there, so household data is duplicated across many rows).
        This skews the mean when not accounting for the multilevel structure.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-mics-wrong-level">
      <title>What goes wrong when you ignore the levels</title>

      <p>
        Suppose you want to know whether urban children are better nourished
        than rural children.
        The child's urban or rural status comes from their household's cluster,
        which is a cluster-level variable.
        The nutritional outcome is a child-level variable.
        These live at different levels of the hierarchy.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you ignore the levels and simply merge the cluster variable onto
        every child row and run an analysis treating each child as an
        independent observation, you will get an answer, but it will be
        overconfident.
        Children in the same cluster share a location, the same local food
        system, the same water supply, and the same local health services.
        They are not independent of each other.
        Treating them as if they were means your analysis thinks it has more
        independent information than it actually does, and confidence intervals
        that should be wide will appear narrow.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is the core problem that multilevel structure creates, and that
        multilevel methods are designed to address.
        The Berkeley admissions example from the opening of this chapter showed
        one consequence: a conclusion that reversed completely when the group
        structure was acknowledged.
        The MICS data shows another: even when the conclusion does not reverse,
        ignoring groups produces false precision.
        Both are reasons to map the levels before beginning any analysis.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section>
    <title>Preparing Multilevel Data for Analysis</title>

    <p>
      Before analysing multilevel data, several preparation steps are essential:
      <ul>
        <li>
          Identify and verify the linking keys: The variable(s) that uniquely
          identify each unit and link levels (e.g., household ID, cluster ID).
        </li>

        <li>
          Check for orphan records: Child records that have no matching
          household record (or vice versa).
          These often indicate data entry or merging errors.
        </li>

        <li>
          Understand the sampling design: MICS surveys use complex multi-stage
          cluster sampling.
          Analysis should account for this through survey weights and
          appropriate software (e.g., R’s survey package or R-Instat’s survey
          functions).
        </li>

        <li>
          Decide the analytical level: Is the unit of analysis the child, the
          household, or the cluster?
          This determines how variables are aggregated or disaggregated.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 3: what makes data multilevel                            -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-what-is-multilevel">
    <title>What Makes Data Multilevel</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Both stories share the same shape, once you strip away the subject
        matter.
        In each, individuals (applicants, patients) belong to a group (a
        department, a stone-size category), and it is the group that determines
        both the individual's chances and how common that individual's situation
        is in the data overall.
        Ignore the grouping, and a comparison across individuals quietly turns
        into a comparison across groups instead, without telling you it has done
        so.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-levels-everywhere">
      <title>Levels are everywhere once you look for them</title>

      <p>
        Neither Berkeley nor the kidney stone study is an unusual dataset.
        Once you start looking for this structure, it is the norm rather than
        the exception:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Pupils belong to schools, which belong to sub-counties, which
              belong to counties.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Patients belong to clinics, which belong to a county health
              system.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Farmers manage plots, which belong to a cooperative or a region.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Survey respondents belong to households, which belong to a sampled
              cluster.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        In every case there is more than one <term>level</term> at which the
        data can honestly be described: the individual level (a pupil, a
        patient, a plot, a person), and one or more group levels above it (a
        school, a clinic, a cooperative, a cluster).
        A number computed at the wrong level, or a group level collapsed away
        entirely, is exactly the mistake both stories in this chapter made.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-multilevel-not-a-trick">
      <title>This is not a trick to watch out for occasionally</title>

      <p>
        It is tempting to file Simpson's paradox away as something that springs
        on you once in a career, if at all.
        Both stories in this chapter argue the opposite.
        Whenever your data has structure (and most real data does!) that
        structure is either going to inform your analysis, or it is going to
        quietly undermine it.
        There is rarely a neutral option to simply ignore it.
      </p>

      <p>
        The rest of this module works with data that has exactly this kind of
        structure.
        Learning to notice it, name it, and check whether it matters before
        trusting a single overall number is the skill this chapter exists to
        start building.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  EXERCISES                                                        -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-multilevel-data">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-identify-observational-unit">
      <title>What is being measured?</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          For each of the following datasets, identify the observational unit:
          that is, what each row of the data represents, and say whether the
          data has a multilevel structure.
          If it does, describe the levels.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              A dataset from the Kenya Demographic and Health Survey, where each
              row records the age, education level, and county of residence of
              one woman interviewed in the survey.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              A dataset of exam results, where each row records the score of one
              student, along with the name of the school the student attends and
              the county the school is in.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              A dataset of daily rainfall measurements, where each row records
              the amount of rainfall at one weather station on one day, along
              with the station's location and elevation.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-aggregation-consequences">
      <title>What gets lost when you aggregate?</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A researcher has data on student exam scores from 20 schools across 3
          counties in Kenya.
          She computes the average score across all students in the dataset and
          reports it as the county average.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              What has she implicitly assumed by computing a single average
              across all students?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Give one example of a situation where this assumption would lead
              her to the wrong conclusion.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              What would she need to do differently to produce a valid
              county-level average?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-simpsons-paradox-spot">
      <title>Spotting the hidden variable</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          A hospital reports that its overall patient survival rate improved
          from 72% in 2020 to 78% in 2022.
          A journalist writes a story praising the hospital's improved care.
        </p>

        <p>
          A data analyst then breaks the survival rate down by ward and finds
          the following:
        </p>

        <tabular>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>Ward</cell>
            <cell>2020 survival rate</cell>
            <cell>2022 survival rate</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>General</cell>
            <cell>90%</cell>
            <cell>88%</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>Intensive Care</cell>
            <cell>50%</cell>
            <cell>48%</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Survival rates went down in both wards between 2020 and 2022.
              How is it possible that the overall survival rate went up?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              What is the hidden variable that the journalist's story ignored?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              What does this example have in common with the Berkeley admissions
              case discussed earlier in this chapter?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-multilevel-kuccps">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <xref ref="ss-kenya-relevance"/> notes that KUCCPS applies an
          Affirmative Action Criteria covering gender when placing students into
          degree programmes.
          Suppose a single national placement rate showed a gap between men and
          women.
          Reasoning the way the Berkeley statisticians did, what grouping
          variable would you want that national rate broken down by before
          deciding whether the gap reflected unfair treatment, rather than
          differences in <em>where</em> applicants applied?
          What pattern in that breakdown would tell you the gap was a
          Simpson's-paradox-style artefact rather than a real one?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-multilevel-kidney-recap">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In your own words, explain why the newer kidney stone procedure looked
          better overall but worse within every group defined by stone size.
          Your explanation should mention <em>which</em> patients were more
          likely to receive which treatment, not just that the numbers
          <q>reversed.</q>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>